        "original_id": "d3eff94d-b396-59e4-8c2b-0300d9b58268",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: Vanderbilt University, Emory University, Methodist Episcopal Church\n- People: Cornelius Vanderbilt, John Emory, William Ridley Wills\n- Places: Oxford, Georgia, metropolitan Atlanta, Brownsville, Tennessee, Tennessee Governor's Mansion\n- Topics: university founding, naming, relocation, endowment, private residence construction\n\nFacts:\n- Emory University was founded as Emory College in 1836 in Oxford, Georgia by the Methodist Episcopal Church and was named in honor of Methodist bishop John Emory.\n- Vanderbilt University was founded in 1873 and was named in honor of Cornelius Vanderbilt, who provided the school its initial $1 million endowment despite never having been to the South.\n- William Ridley Wills was born in Brownsville, Tennessee on March 4, 1897.\n- In 1915, Emory College relocated to metropolitan Atlanta and was rechartered as Emory University.\n- William Ridley Wills graduated from Vanderbilt University and was a member of the Fugitive literary group.\n- The Tennessee Governor's Mansion is a three-story Georgian-style mansion that was built in 1929 as a private home for William Ridley Wills and his family.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
